It doesn't have any way to see if its going to do in the future so constant monitoring, 
Rootkit = Hides in the OS Kernel part of the OS so its hard to find. Look for unusable anti malware scans. Invisible to antivirus but could be discovered after its                   infected then find/build software to pick it up. Secure boot with UEFI (secure in the BIOS)
          Hiding in the kernel and being invisible to normal antivirus is exactly right. Think of it as malware that has disguised itself as part of the building’s                   foundations.
Ransomware: the main focus. Ransomware encrypts your files so you can’t open them, then demands payment, usually in cryptocurrency, to unlock them.

Keylogger: It records everything typed: passwords, messages, bank details. 
It captures what you type before it gets encrypted, so even a secure HTTPS site doesn’t protect you. The keylogger already has it.
It sends the captured data back to the attacker, so a firewall watching for unusual outbound traffic can help catch it, along with anti-malware. MFA also limits the damage, because a stolen password alone isn’t enough.

logic bomb: Can be triggered by a time OR an event. Time bomb: goes off at a set date and time, like your example.
Event trigger: goes off when something happens. The classic exam scenario is a disgruntled IT employee who plants code to wipe the servers if their name is ever removed from payroll. Logic bombs are hard to detect because there’s often no known signature to match. Messer’s defences are formal change control processes (so no one can quietly add code) and monitoring for unexpected changes. 2013 example. It was the attack on South Korean banks and broadcasters, where machines were wiped and ATMs went down.

Rootkit: once a rootkit is identified, specialised rootkit removal tools can be built to find and remove that particular one. And yes, Secure Boot with UEFI is the big defence. It checks that the operating system hasn’t been tampered with before it’s allowed to start, so a modified kernel won’t load.

How to protect against ransomware? Backups: the number one defence. Backing up operating systems and applications as well as data, and having spare machines ready to go, is real business-continuity thinking. Just remember the key exam word: backups should be offline or separate, so the ransomware can’t encrypt them too.
Analogy: someone breaks into your house, puts all your belongings in a locked cage, and leaves a note: pay up and we’ll send the key. Your things are still there, you just can’t use them, and paying doesn’t guarantee you’ll get the key. Keep them offline or separate, otherwise the ransomware encrypts your backups too.
Keep everything updated: operating system, applications and antivirus. Anti-malware software: keep it running and up to date.

